1
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
The amazing drama
you're about to see

2
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:18,000
is a matter of human record.

3
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
You may believe it or not,

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
but the real people
who lived this story,

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
they believe it; they know.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,000
They took that...
one step beyond.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,000
ANNOUNCER:
Alcoa presents...

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,000
a new and unusual kind
of television program

9
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
that takes you just beyond
the world in which you live.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Alcoa presents aluminum from
the world's leading producer--

11
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Aluminum Company of America--

12
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,000
who creates new and unusual
uses of this wondrous metal

13
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
for the world
in which you do live.

14
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:04,000
And now John Newland takes you
one step beyond.

15
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,000
use,

16
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
early April, 1912,

17
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:17,000
began the bizarre and untold
chapter of a news story

18
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
that would soon stun
the civilized world.

19
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,000
In this library
was an unread book.

20
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Coming events cast
their shadows before them...?

21
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,000
Maybe.

22
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Remember this book.

23
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,000
We'll come back to it later.

24
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
It might shock you.

25
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:40,000
(woman screaming)

26
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,000
(screaming continues)

27
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Mother.

28
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Mother?

29
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Mother.

30
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Mother!

31
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Mother!

32
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:13,000
What is it?

33
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,000
What's wrong, dear?

34
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,000
There was water,
dark water.

35
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:18,000
I was drowning.

36
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:19,000
I couldn't swim anymore.

37
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:20,000
I was drowning.

38
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Oh, you just had one of those
old-fashioned nightmares.

39
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Oh, no...
I don't blame you.

40
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,000
All this excitement
about the wedding.

41
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:28,000
No wonder...

42
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Eric... Eric was there

43
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
and I couldn't find
him in the dark.

44
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Then the water
closed in over me.

45
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,000
What does it mean?

46
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Why, nothing.

47
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Absolutely nothing.

48
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
And in four days,
you'll be married

49
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
and in Switzerland
on your honeymoon--

50
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
the happiest bride
that ever was.

51
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
And you can't drown
in Switzerland.

52
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Nobody ever does.

53
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
All they do is bang into trees
on those foolish skis.

54
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,000
But drowning-- no.

55
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,000
It was all so clear to me.

56
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,000
And I could... feel the water.

57
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,000
It was like ice.

58
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Now, if you must dream,

59
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,000
I order you to dream only
pleasant things--

60
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Eric, your wedding dress

61
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:27,000
and how happy
you're going to be.

62
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Promise?

63
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
That's better.

64
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Now, come on back to bed.

65
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:34,000
But I couldn't find him.

66
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:35,000
I couldn't find him...

67
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Now that is enough,
darling.

68
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,000
And I shouldn't worry
Eric with this.

69
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,000
He's been trying so hard

70
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:45,000
to make everything
wonderful for you.

71
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,000
There.

72
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,000
Now...

73
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,000
good night, dear.

74
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:57,000
And no more nightmares.

75
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Good night.

76
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Good morning, Emily.

77
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
Is the future Mrs. Farley about?

78
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Grace?!

79
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,000
GRACE:
Eric.

80
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:41,000
I know it's early

81
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:43,000
but I wanted to see
what my wife

82
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
would look like
in the mornings.

83
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Ugly.

84
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Would you like some coffee,
darling?

85
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:49,000
I'd love some.

86
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,000
You like surprises,
don't you, darling?

87
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:52,000
I love them.

88
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Come on. Surprise me.

89
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:55,000
It's about our honeymoon.

90
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Our honeymoon?

91
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,000
We're not going
to Switzerland.

92
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,000
We're not?
Well, where are we going?

93
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:01,000
We're going to America...

94
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:02,000
to New York City.

95
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
New York?

96
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Eric.

97
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Now, why don't you ask me
how we're going to get there?

98
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:10,000
Swim?

99
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:11,000
Rowboat? Raft?

100
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,000
All right, darling, how?

101
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:15,000
That's how.

102
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Stateroom 111b,
Boat Deck--

103
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:18,000
right next door

104
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:21,000
to Mr. And Mrs. John
Jacob Astor, no less--

105
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,000
on the maiden voyage of His
Royal Majesty's Ship Titanic,

106
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Southampton to New York.

107
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
Six days at sea,
next Tuesday.

108
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
At sea?

109
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Oh, just a little
sugar, darling.

110
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:37,000
I can't.

111
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,000
I... I can't believe it.

112
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,000
I can hardly
believe it myself,

113
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
but the office
has connections.

114
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
Oh.

115
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,000
Oh, you lucky children.

116
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,000
You sure you won't
miss Switzerland?

117
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
Oh, Switzerland will keep,

118
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,000
but the Titanic,
our honeymoon...

119
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
that's once in a lifetime.

120
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:02,000
The paper's full
of nothing but the Titanic.

121
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Listen to this, Grace:

122
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:05,000
"R.M.S. Titanic,
the world's largest

123
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,000
"and most luxurious liner,

124
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
"is writing a new
and glamorous chapter

125
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
"in man's conquest
of the sea.

126
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
"by virtue of her five
watertight compartments,

127
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:20,000
"she's being hailed in
marine engineering circles

128
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:22,000
"as the unsinkable ship.

129
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,000
"Her first passenger list
makes her a floating home

130
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
"for royalty, statesmen,
international society

131
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
"and stars of the theater
and the arts.

132
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:32,000
And... "

133
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
and Mr. And Mrs.
Eric Farley

134
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,000
who are absolutely
unknown.

135
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:41,000
And absolutely in love.

136
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:53,000
Grace?

137
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Grace?!

138
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Grace, what is it, darling?

139
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:13,000
What is it?

140
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:15,000
Darling...

141
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:16,000
we mustn't...

142
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,000
we can't go on that ship.

143
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,000
It happened again.

144
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:21,000
I dreamed...

145
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,000
Now, now, darling, I
do wish you wouldn't...

146
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,000
I saw the sea,
the cold sea.

147
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:29,000
There were people struggling
in the water...

148
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:30,000
Really.

149
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,000
Sinking... dying...

150
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Grace.

151
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
And then a huge ship
slid down!

152
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
Down!

153
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:40,000
Now, now, it's just
a bad dream.

154
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:43,000
Besides, that ship
couldn't be the Titanic.

155
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
The Titanic can't sink.

156
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:46,000
Everyone knows that.

157
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:49,000
I saw a lifeboat tossing
in the water.

158
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:51,000
There were letters
on the side of it.

159
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
I saw, so clearly I saw,
the word Titanic

160
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:54,000
on the side of the boat!

161
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,000
I did. I did.

162
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:57,000
I did!

163
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:02,000
(sobbing):
I did! I did! I did! I did!

164
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
Just what I've always wanted.

165
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,000
Only my own dear Aunt Agatha
would've guessed.

166
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,000
Have you ever kept birds,
my darling?

167
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:21,000
No. Neither have I.

168
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
You ever thought
of keeping birds?

169
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
Neither have I.

170
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:27,000
Then by all means,
write dear Aunt Agatha

171
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
and tell her she's made us
very, very happy.

172
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:34,000
I will.

173
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:46,000
I know all about last night
and the night before that.

174
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:48,000
Mother told you?

175
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:50,000
I suppose you think
I've gone off my head--

176
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:53,000
one of those silly,
hysterical women.

177
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:55,000
I think you're a lot overtired
and a little over-imaginative.

178
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
But this isn't my imagination.

179
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
Believe me, I saw it.
I saw it.

180
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Now, that is silly.

181
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,000
You don't believe what
you see in your sleep.

182
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:04,000
I mean, unless
you're a gypsy.

183
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:07,000
Grace...

184
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
do you trust me?

185
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
You know I do.

186
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
Then don't spoil
the happiest time

187
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:16,000
of our whole lives just
because of a bad dream.

188
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:19,000
Everything's going to be
all right, I promise.

189
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
I'll take care
of you, always.

190
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
Oh, hold me tight.

191
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:24,000
Oh, darling.

192
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:28,000
You know, if I didn't
know you so well,

193
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:29,000
I'd think that you'd fallen
in love with another man--

194
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
that you didn't
want to marry me.

195
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:36,000
I couldn't have
anybody but you.

196
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:37,000
Very well, I believe you.

197
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,000
Only remember-- if there
ever is another man,

198
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:45,000
I was one of the best shots
in the Cold Stream Guards.

199
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
Are you asleep?

200
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:17,000
Hmm-mm.

201
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
I think I will keep
a bird after all.

202
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:22,000
I beg your pardon.

203
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:26,000
That would please
your Aunt Agatha, wouldn't it?

204
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:27,000
Terribly.

205
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:31,000
Did you have any particular
species in mind?

206
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:32,000
Oh, a lovebird,
of course.

207
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:37,000
Oh, naturally,
a lovebird.

208
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
Thank you, darling.

209
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,000
What for?

210
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:51,000
The six most perfect days
of my life.

211
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,000
How lovely you are.

212
00:10:58,000 --> 00:10:59,000
And very young.

213
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:04,000
There'll be even better days.

214
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:09,000
You've... forgiven me,
haven't you

215
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:10,000
for those silly scenes I made?

216
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
I've forgotten the whole thing.

217
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:16,000
I'm glad.

218
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
Such awful...

219
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
ridiculous dreams.

220
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,000
It wasn't a dream at all.

221
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
I was wide awake when I felt

222
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
there was something wrong
with the ship. Quite wrong.

223
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
I know you'll laugh.

224
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
And I don't blame you.

225
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
I was in my cabin
this afternoon--

226
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:38,000
I imagine it was around 4:00--

227
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,000
when I heard
this terrible sound.

228
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:42,000
What kind of sound?

229
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:45,000
It was a terrible
grinding sound

230
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:48,000
as though the ship had suddenly
struck some immovable object.

231
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
The whole boat seemed to
tremble with the impact.

232
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:55,000
Tell me, by any chance, did you
hear anything or feel anything?

233
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:58,000
No, no, I heard nothing
like that, I'm happy to say.

234
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,000
You know, it's probably
your imagination.

235
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:04,000
Or, uh, more likely,
indigestion.

236
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
Why don't you see
the ship's doctor.

237
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:07,000
He's a good man I'm told.

238
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
Oh, I'm all right physically.

239
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:10,000
It's just that...

240
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:14,000
for the first time in my
life, I'm... I'm afraid.

241
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
I'm afraid even for
the next few hours.

242
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
I don't know
what I'm afraid of.

243
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:22,000
It's as though something
in my... my soul.

244
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
You know, if I weren't
such a hard-headed realist

245
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:29,000
when I were at home, I think I'd
call on my minister.

246
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,000
Four...

247
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:39,000
Four, six.

248
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:45,000
Four... four...

249
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:50,000
six.

250
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,000
Four...

251
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:02,000
four, six.

252
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
(knocking)

253
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
Excuse me, Dr. Morgan,

254
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:09,000
but it's nearly time
for the service.

255
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:13,000
Miss Parsons, I'd like
the congregation

256
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:17,000
to sing Hymn number
446 tonight.

257
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
But, Dr. Morgan,
the hymns for tonight

258
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:20,000
are already posted in the church

259
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
and the organist...

260
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
Please.

261
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
Four... four-six.

262
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,000
I didn't know that was
one of your favorite hymns.

263
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:30,000
It isn't.

264
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
I don't even know
the words.

265
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:37,000
I simply know
that we must sing it tonight.

266
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,000
"Hear, Father,
while we pray to thee

267
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:50,000
for those in peril on the sea."

268
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:53,000
Better we should be praying
for our farmers...

269
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:58,000
after a winter like this.

270
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:16,000
Evening.

271
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:42,000
CONGREGATION:
♪ Hear, Father,
while we pray to thee ♪

272
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:47,000
♪ For those in peril
on the sea ♪

273
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:52,000
♪ Thy grace and bounty,
we ask for them ♪

274
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:57,000
♪ See them safely home again

275
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:04,000
♪ Hear, dear Father,
while we pray to thee ♪

276
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:08,000
♪ For those in peril
on the sea ♪

277
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:13,000
♪ Thy grace and bounty,
we ask for them... ♪

278
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
It's funny.

279
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
At sea, one almost forgets
it's Sunday.

280
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:20,000
♪ ...home again.

281
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,000
What is it, darling?

282
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:25,000
Nothing.

283
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
Why, you're cold.

284
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:32,000
Come to bed.

285
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
Harry Teller,
you're the only man

286
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
in New York City
that's still up.

287
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:41,000
You've been here
for four hours.

288
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:45,000
I don't know what's
wrong with me.

289
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:48,000
Oh, Harry, what's this?

290
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
I thought you were
supposed to be drawing

291
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
pretty girls
in the new spring styles.

292
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:55,000
You'll never make
the deadline now.

293
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
I don't know
how it happened.

294
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
I was...

295
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:02,000
I don't know, helpless.

296
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:07,000
Something seemed to
be guiding my hand.

297
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:12,000
I can't explain it.

298
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
You're tired.

299
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:17,000
You've been
working too hard.

300
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
Now, come on.

301
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:22,000
No.

302
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
No, it's not that.

303
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:29,000
Harry, let's go to bed.

304
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
You know, this drawing...

305
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:36,000
it's awful.

306
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:39,000
I don't know
who would want it.

307
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:40,000
But you know something?

308
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:43,000
This is the best work
you've ever done.

309
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:46,000
I've never seen you do
anything like this before.

310
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:51,000
All of this detail
in here and...

311
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:54,000
Harry, what is it?

312
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
Your hands are like ice.

313
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:00,000
The water in the
drawing was cold.

314
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:05,000
Icy cold.

315
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:22,000
(foghorn blowing)

316
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:26,000
What the devil are you doing?

317
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:40,000
Grace?

318
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:44,000
Grace?

319
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:49,000
Grace?

320
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:54,000
Grace...

321
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
What happened?

322
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:57,000
What's the matter?

323
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
Everything's
all right now.

324
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:00,000
Come back to bed.

325
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:03,000
I don't understand.
I was sleeping.

326
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:07,000
Someone was screaming hurry,
that we need warm clothes

327
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
and I must...

328
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
What's wrong with me?

329
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:14,000
I must be
losing my mind.

330
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
Oh...

331
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
I'm losing my mind.

332
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:19,000
(gasping)

333
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:22,000
Snap out of it, Grace.

334
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
You're driving me
out of mine, you know.

335
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
Look, I'm not
John Jacob Astor

336
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:29,000
or a member
of the nobility.

337
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
I've saved for two years
for this trip,

338
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:34,000
wanting it to be
a perfect honeymoon

339
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:39,000
and to have it spoiled
by such childish nonsense...

340
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:41,000
Oh, I'm...

341
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,000
I'm sorry, Eric.

342
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:46,000
I'm sorry.

343
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
All right, darling,
you're forgiven.

344
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
Just don't make a habit

345
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:55,000
of pitching my best clothes
on the floor.

346
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:05,000
Iceberg.

347
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:06,000
Iceberg dead ahead!

348
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
Iceberg dead ahead!

349
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:11,000
Iceberg dead ahead!

350
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:15,000
(horn blowing)

351
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:20,000
(deep groaning and rumbling)

352
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:32,000
Engine seems
to have stopped.

353
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:38,000
You all right?

354
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,000
Oh, Steward...

355
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:44,000
Sir?

356
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
The engines seem
to have stopped.
What was it?

357
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:49,000
There was some report
of an iceberg, sir.

358
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
The captain stopped
the vessel, sir,

359
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
just to be
on the safe side, you know.

360
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
What an incredible ship.

361
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:56,000
What service.

362
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
They run out of ice
at the bar

363
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
so what happens?
We hit an iceberg.

364
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
Good, fresh ice;
that's what I like.

365
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
It's all over the deck.

366
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:05,000
Have a drink, chum.

367
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:10,000
Uh, no, thank you.

368
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
Never seen an
iceberg, darling.

369
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:14,000
Shall we go
and have a look?

370
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:15,000
That's a good idea.

371
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
We'll wrap up
good and warm.

372
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
(people clamoring outside)

373
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:20,000
(knocking)

374
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:21,000
STEWARD:
Captain's orders.

375
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:22,000
Go to your
lifeboat station.

376
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:23,000
Please dress warmly.

377
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
There's no need
to panic.

378
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:26,000
Just take it
nice and easy.

379
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:29,000
It's just a precaution, darling.

380
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:30,000
Remember what the captain
said tonight--

381
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:35,000
the ship that nothing
in the world could sink?

382
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
What are you doing?

383
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:47,000
Trying to impress an iceberg
with your jewels?

384
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
Just in case we might
not be coming back.

385
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:52,000
Oh, come on.

386
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:57,000
(clanging)

387
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:05,000
(horn blowing)

388
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:12,000
Easy does it, now.

389
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
Women and children only.

390
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
The men will follow later.

391
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:21,000
There we go.

392
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
Right.

393
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:26,000
There's a little one.

394
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:27,000
There's a young fellow.

395
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:28,000
Take care of
him, will you?

396
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
Yes, sir.

397
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:31,000
GRACE:
No!

398
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:33,000
Thank you, madam.

399
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:35,000
No. No! I won't
go without you!

400
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:36,000
Hush, my
darling, hush.

401
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:37,000
No!

402
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:38,000
Listen to me.

403
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
Have you forgotten your
wedding vows so soon?

404
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:43,000
Love, honor, obey?

405
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
Now, you go with
the other women

406
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:49,000
in boat four.

407
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:51,000
Oh, I...

408
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:52,000
I'm sorry.

409
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:55,000
I love our marriage--

410
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:57,000
all six days, five hours
and 20 minutes of it.

411
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
I even loved our first fight.

412
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:02,000
If you ever have
another bad dream,

413
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:03,000
I'll listen to you--
every word.

414
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:05,000
Come, come,
madam.

415
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:10,000
It's all right,
it's all right.

416
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:16,000
Good night, my love.

417
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:37,000
Lower away!

418
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:01,000
Eric Farley
never came back to this house.

419
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:04,000
Over 1,500 people perished
that dreadful night

420
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:05,000
aboard the Titanic.

421
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:08,000
Eric Farley was among them.

422
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:11,000
You've seen some of the
unexplained, supernatural events

423
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:14,000
surrounding the life and death
of a great ship.

424
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:18,000
Now, do you remember this book?

425
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:21,000
It's a novel published in 1898,

426
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:25,000
14 years before the ill-fated
vessel was even dreamed of.

427
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:27,000
The title: Futility.

428
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:29,000
Its author: Morgan Robertson.

429
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,000
It's a story

430
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:35,000
about the largest liner
ever built

431
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:38,000
carrying on its maiden voyage
the rich and great of the world.

432
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:43,000
Then, on an April night,
it struck an iceberg.

433
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:48,000
The vessel in this book
was 70,000 tons.

434
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:51,000
The Titanic: 66,000 tons.

435
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:55,000
The length of the ship
in these pages: 800 feet.

436
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:58,000
The Titanic: 882 feet.

437
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:03,000
Both made 25 knots, both carried
about 3,000 passengers

438
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
both liners had lifeboats

439
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:08,000
for only a fraction
of that number

440
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:12,000
and both were considered
unsinkable.

441
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:13,000
Oh...

442
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:15,000
and one more thing.

443
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:17,000
The author called his ship...

444
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:21,000
the Titan.

445
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:26,000
In a moment,
something else to think about.

446
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:34,000
This is the ticket
that carried a passenger--

447
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:37,000
Arthur Douglas by name--
from New York to Chicago,

448
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:39,000
on a fantastic excursion
into terror.

449
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:41,000
Now, if you were
one of the passengers

450
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:43,000
on the train that night,

451
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:46,000
we'll thank you to keep
your secret until next week,

452
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
when a perfectly normal man
takes that...

453
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:51,000
one step beyond.
